"[2512.19609] MapTrace: Scalable Data Generation for Route Tracing on Maps (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.19609)\nciteturn31882view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn31881view0\",\"id\":39}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 22 Dec 2025]\nL9: # Title:MapTrace: Scalable Data Generation for Route Tracing on Maps\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Artemis Panagopoulou , cite6†Aveek Purohit , cite7†Achin Kulshrestha , cite8†Soroosh Yazdani , cite9†Mohit Goyal L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled MapTrace: Scalable Data Generation for Route Tracing on Maps, by Artemis Panagopoulou and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:While Multimodal Large Language Models have achieved human-like performance on many visual and textual reasoning tasks, their proficiency in fine-grained spatial understanding, such as route tracing on maps remains limited. Unlike humans, who can quickly learn to parse and navigate maps, current models often fail to respect fundamental path constraints, in part due to the prohibitive cost and difficulty of collecting large-scale, pixel-accurate path annotations.\nL17: To address this, we introduce a scalable synthetic data generation pipeline that leverages synthetic map images and pixel-level parsing to automatically produce precise annotations for this challenging task. Using this pipeline, we construct a fine-tuning dataset of 23k path samples across 4k maps, enabling models to acquire more human-like spatial capabilities. Using this dataset, we fine-tune both open-source and proprietary MLLMs.\nL18: Results on MapBench show that finetuning substantially improves robustness, raising success rates by up to 6.4 points, while also reducing path-tracing error (NDTW). These gains highlight that fine-grained spatial reasoning, absent in pretrained models, can be explicitly taught with synthetic supervision.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2512.19609 [cs.CV]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2512.19609v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.19609†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Artemis Panagopoulou [cite15†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Mon, 22 Dec 2025 17:45:39 UTC (3,689 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled MapTrace: Scalable Data Generation for Route Tracing on Maps, by Artemis Panagopoulou and 4 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite10†View PDF L35:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite16†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CV\nL42: \nL43: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L44: \nL45: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-12 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite23†cs L50: cite24†cs.AI L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL74: \nL75: Bibliographic Explorer (cite28†What is the Explorer?†inf